  "overview": {
    "text": "A double bond is a covalent bond in which two atoms share two pairs of electrons—four electrons altogether. In the usual bonding model, it consists of one sigma (σ) bond and one pi (π) bond. A Lewis structure represents it with two lines, as in C=C, but the two parts differ: the sigma bond forms through head-on orbital overlap, while the pi bond forms through sideways overlap of p orbitals, creating electron density above and below the line between the nuclei. In ethene (C₂H₄), this arrangement helps keep the molecule planar; twisting the atoms would disrupt the pi-bond overlap. A triple bond, by comparison, contains one sigma bond and two pi bonds.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/1-8-sp2-hybrid-orbitals-and-the-structure-of-ethylene",
      "https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Introductory_Chemistry/Introductory_Chemistry_(CK-12)/09:_Covalent_Bonding/9.24:_Sigma_and_Pi_Bonds",
      "https://openstax.org/books/chemistry/pages/8-3-multiple-bonds"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
